\title{16th Sunday after Pentecost 2026}
\tag{cmc}
\date{2026-09-13}
\author{liamoc}
\quote{
Da pacem Dómine, sustinéntibus te, ut prophétæ tui fidéles inveniántur: exáudi preces servi tui, et plebis tuæ Israel.
}

\p{I sang again at [St. Michael and all Saints](https://smas.church) in the morning where we sang Palestrina's [Missa Brevis](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Tku8ceGJ4dk) and Wesley's [Wash me throughly](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0r3BkuxjD6k): }
\quote{
  \poem{
    \line{Wash me throughly from my wickedness}
\line{And forgive me all my sin}
\line{For I acknowledge my faults}
\line{And my sin is ever before me}}
}
\p{I also joined the choir at [Old St. Paul's](https://www.osp.org.uk) once again for Evensong and Benediction. We sang canticles by Harold Darke (both [Magnificat](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vNL2_M6LEaU) and [Nunc Dimittis](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XYwjopHRo_o)), and an anthem by our own John Kitchen: [Stand, Holy Cross](https://youtu.be/cIdwDZPLfRQ?t=2512), a setting of a text by Gordon Graham (link is to the livestream of the service): }
\quote{\poem{
  \line{Stand, Holy Cross, between my God and me}
  \line{And cast a shadow where my heart can hide}
  \line{Its frail mortality}
  \line{From his perspicuous light.}
  \line{\br}
  \line{The Cross's shade supplies my sin's disguise.}
  \line{My soul protected, I can turn my eyes;}
  \line{And now the Father’s light allows me view:}
  \line{A world, my world, made new.}
  \line{\br}
  \line{Then as horizons widen to a farther shore}
  \line{And light on light enlightens more and more,}
  \line{This larger vision brings a deeper peace}
  \line{And sin and sorrow cease.}
  \line{\br}
  \line{Stand, Holy Cross, between my God and me.}
}}
